\par\par \b \uc2  Israel Shekel Up Again, Allaying Depreciation Fears \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1997\par 16:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (AP-Dow Jones)--The shekel strengthened against the dollar for a third straight day Wednesday, easing fears of a steep depreciation that had been fueled by the recent widening of the trading band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the shekel at 3.5050 to the dollar, a rise of 1.24% from Tuesday's close of 3.5490 shekels. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.4950 to 3.53 to the dollar, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. About half an hour after the 1230 fixing the shekel was trading within a range of 3.5170 to 3.5270 shekels. \par \par \uc2  Trading volume was moderate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel slid some 5% in the week after the trading band was widened June 18, generating fears of a much bigger depreciation that would feed inflation and lead to a rise in the 12.7% discount rate. \par \par \uc2  But the shekel's slide came to a halt Monday, and so far this week the shekel has regained nearly half of the value it lost last week. \par \par \uc2  'People reached the conclusion that the dollar was going down,' said Ilan Zadka, a dealer at United Mizrahi Bank. 'Customers stopped buying dollars on signals from the Bank of Israel and top advisers to the prime minister that they don't welcome a strong dollar and that if the dollar continued to rise, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by raising the discount rate or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade.' \par \par \uc2  It took relatively few sales Wednesday to bring the exchange rate down considerably, he said. \par \par \uc2  Still, even though fears of a steep depreciation have been allayed, banks were still nervous Wednesday, as evidenced by the spread, dealers said. They had no specific explanation for the banks' jumpiness. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 02-07-97 \par \par \uc2  1403GMT \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7204rdz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
