\par\par \b \uc2  Analysts see quiet after the storm for shekel. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, June 30 (Reuter) - Analysts say they are still waiting for the dust to clear after the Israeli government reformed the exchange rate mechanism and the Bank of Israel slashed interest rates two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  Since the cabinet decision, which widened the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band and lowered its crawling slope, the Israeli shekel has lost some five percent against the dollar to 3.587 on Monday. The Tel Aviv Stock Exchange's Mishtanim index of 100 leading has fallen 3.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Both the stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been reacting to the uncertainty, not to the fundamentals," said Nadine Baudot-Trajtenberg, senior economist in the securities division of Israel's Bank Hapoalim. \par \par \uc2  Like most other analysts, she discounts the long-term significance of the changes. \par \par \uc2  "We believe the shekel rate will stabilise in the short run," said Vikas Nath, emerging markets strategist at UBS in London. "We are shooting for 3.47 or 3.48 once the short cover gives way." \par \par \uc2  By year end, he sees the dollar at 3.55-3.6 shekels, not far off from the 3.5 shekels he forecast before the eruption. \par \par \uc2  The shekel's current slide marks a reversal for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had been riding high on the back of big capital inflows into the country. Some of the inflow was due to stepped-up foreign investment, but most was due to Israelis' taking advantage of lower overseas interest rates. \par \par \uc2  That is why Baudot-Trajtenberg says the shekel's weakening is a short-term trend. \par \par \uc2  "The people who've forced the depreciation are mostly the business sector here," she said. "But you don't get a sense that they want to get out of their dollar loans." \par \par \uc2  She expects the shekel to experience periodic waves of speculation but settle near the lower end of its trading band against the basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to which it is linked. As of Sunday, the shekel was 4.6 percent above the lower band. \par \par \uc2  But Hezy Gutman, chief economist at First International Bank of Israel, warns that each day the shekel falls further the odds of a significant rebound grow slimmer. Gutman said he is not confident that the shekel will appreciate enough to again reach the lower end of the band. \par \par \uc2  Gutman said he could still see the shekel falling far enough for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "There are situations where you have no choice," he said. "If the depreciation reaches 10 percent, (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) can create a balance with just a little \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  However, a top Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  official indicated on Sunday that the bank would be more inclined to raise interest rates than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The exchange rate has an outsized impact on the trade-oriented Israeli economy. A weaker shekel could give a boost to the country's sluggish exports but also feed into inflation by raising the price of imports and housing. Israeli home prices are customarily linked to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But economists are cautious in assessing its impact. \par \par \uc2  Nath said even a moderate weakening of the shekel over then long run would improve export prospects. But Gutman said the continued uncertainty overhanging the exchange rate would moderate any export growth. \par \par \uc2  Israel's sluggish growth and rising unemployment would discourage importers and builders from passing along the higher prices created by the weaker shekel, economists say. As a result, inflation is unlikely to accelerate. \par \par \uc2  But Gutman said interest rates are unlikely to fall either, after the Bank of Israel lowered rates 1.2 percentage point to 12.7 percent two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  "My forecast is that we won't see a further fall in the nominal interest rate," said Baudot-Trajtenberg. "I think we might see a rise towards the end of the year." \par \par \uc2  She warned that the budget may come under pressure in the second half of the year at the same time that the Bank of Israel begins orienting monetary policy towards the government's 1998 inflation target. She said the target could be as low as six percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6u034q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
