\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel chief discounts inflation worries. \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, June 26 (Reuter) - Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel said on Thursday that the weakening of the shekel over the past week would not necessarily lead to higher inflation. \par \par \uc2  "There are circumstances that exchange rate changes translate themselves relatively quickly to prices and there are circumstances under which this translation is not as quick," Frenkel told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "It depends very much on the state of the economy, it depends very much on budgetary circumstances and it depends very much on monetary policy," he said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel has fallen sharply since last Friday, shortly after the government announced a reform of the exchange rate regime and the Bank of Israel lowered the base rate by a steep 1.2 percentage points to 12.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  In early trading on Thursday, the dollar was up 1.5 percent against the Israeli \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to about 3.58 shekels. Since last Friday, the dollar has gained about 4.7 percent against the shekel. \par \par \uc2  Israeli inflation has been slowing since April. But the economy has been slowing, too, and this year is expected to show its lowest rate of growth in seven years. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel, who was attending the annual general meeting of the Manufacturers Association trade group, said he did not believe the shekel would continue weakening. "I would not necessarily argue that a depreciation of a few days must continue with further depreciation," he said. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, he indicated that unlike most economists, the bank had not been surprised by the shekel's movement. \par \par \uc2  "Most commentators, politicians and others had fears of a strong appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of Israel had argued that that's not a necessary presumption," Frenkel said. "As a matter of fact, there are good economic reasons for a depreciation." \par \par \uc2  Frenkel would not say whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  would consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem a further depreciation. \par \par \uc2  "There are various assessments," he said. "We at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue following very carefully the markets. I can assure you that no abrupt measures will be taken - the idea is steady as you go." \par \par \uc2  - Tel Aviv newsroom, 972-3-537-2211 x233 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6q03h0i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
