\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel denies rate cut linked to politics. \b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1997\par 18:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM, June 22 (Reuter) - Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel on Sunday denied political considerations influenced his decision to lower interest rates sharply after the cabinet approved changes in the exchange rate regime. \par \par \uc2  "There had been a long period of uncertainty, which created a lot of sensitivity in the money and capital markets. It was very important to end it," Frenkel said, explaining his surprise decision to cut the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key lending rate by 1.2 percentage points last Wednesday to 12.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's only considerations were falling inflation and inflationary expectations and the slowing economy. He also cited the measures taken by the cabinet at an emergency meeting on Wednesday to ease foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and adjust the exchange rate system. \par \par \uc2  "The decision was made only with these factors taken into account -- period," Frenkel said. \par \par \uc2  Frenkel's remarks come amid charges that he had a hand in bringing about Finance Minister Dan Meridor's abrupt resignation last Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel said in a statement the state comptroller had made enquiries about Frenkel's role in the affair. \par \par \uc2  Meridor, a political rival of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, has charged that the timing and method Netanyahu used to bring the decision to the cabinet was meant to embarrass him, leaving him no choice but to resign. \par \par \uc2  Meridor has not specifically charged Frenkel with involvement, but media commentators have suggested the governor aided Netanyahu. \par \par \uc2  In a commentary on Sunday, the daily newspaper Maariv recalled that Frenkel had been a candidate for finance minister in the Netanyahu government and was supposed to head a planned council of economic advisers. \par \par \uc2  "We expected to continue seeing close cooperation between the prime minister's and the governor's office....That reached a peak in the 'Diagonol Affair,' in which the governor joined with the prime minister in helping him to indirectly force out the finance minister," the newspaper said. \par \par \uc2  A spokeswoman for the government watchdog office would not confirm or deny the report. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel was originally due to make its monthly interest rate announcement this Monday, at which time economists had expected only a mild cut of about 0.5 percentage point. Interest rates had been unchanged since the end of February. \par \par \uc2  Early last week, however, speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would succeed in its long-running campaign to widen the diagonal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band created huge speculative pressure on the shekel, forcing the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily. \par \par \uc2  Since the start of the year, the shekel has pressed the lower limit of its trading band against the basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to which it is pegged. The market assumed that if the band was widened the shekel would be free to appreciate. \par \par \uc2  The exchange rate adjustments approved by the cabinet did not affect the lower end of the band but reduced the incline of the slope, enabling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen more than it would have under the previous system. \par \par \uc2  In Friday trading, the dollar unexpectedly gained ground on the shekel, with the representative rate set at 3.437 shekels, up 0.53 percent from Thursday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket also strengthened against the shekel, rising 0.57 percent to 3.7343. \par \par \uc2  There is no foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in Israel on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  - Jerusalem newsroom, 972-2-537-0502 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6m03idq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
