\par\par \b \uc2  Netanyahu in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  talks with Treasury, Cent Bank. \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1997\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CAESAREA, Israel, June 16 (Reuter) - Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu will meet the country's Finance Minister and the Governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday in a bid to hammer out a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liberalisation plan, a government source said. \par \par \uc2  The discussions are also expected to look at widening the Israeli shekel's trading bands. \par \par \uc2  The source said that Netanyahu aimed to take a decisive role in a long running dispute between the Treasury and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on these two issues. He said he doubted the three would reach a decision tonight but expected a resolution over the next few days. \par \par \uc2  "We've had numerous discussions about the subject. Both sides have voiced their positions but a decision must be made," Netanyahu told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The meeting is scheduled for 1700 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The source said that the absence of any decision had made the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets extremely nervous. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel, which has been pressing to widen the shekel's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bands, bought almost $150 million during \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday in an attempt to keep the shekel down, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is allowed to trade in a band of seven percent above or below a diagonal line designed to devalue it by six percent a year. \par \par \uc2  An influx of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to prevent the shekel from strengthening beyond the seven percent limit. \par \par \uc2  The Treasury has refused to countenance a widening of the band for fear that it would cause a further appreciation of the shekel, which it regards as being overvalued. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt6g04307\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
