\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Israel \highlight\uc2  Shekel Unchanged Vs Dlr; Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Israel Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 298 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 1997\par 15:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JERUSALEM (AP-Dow Jones)--The shekel closed unchanged against the dollar Thursday in fairly heavy trading, and the Bank of Israel bought foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the shekel and push it back inside its trading band, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the shekel at 3.3950 to the dollar. Shortly after the fixing, the shekel was trading at 3.3930 to the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The shekel traded within a range of 3.3920 to 3.3970 to the dollar, they said. \par \par \uc2  Trading was dominated by dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  'From the beginning of the week, since the Bank of Israel announced that the discount rate wouldn't be changed, people have been selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' said Ilan Zadka, a dealer at United Mizrahi Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  'Also, toward the end of the month, there are heavy conversions to pay (monthly) salaries,' Zadka said. \par \par \uc2  The conversions have forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading after a hiatus of about three weeks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $50 million in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, if not more. \par \par \uc2  Customers have been taking advantage of Israel's high discount rate - currently 13.9% - to take out loans in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or raise capital abroad and convert the money to shekels. \par \par \uc2  The capital inflows have strengthened the shekel, and in the first three months of the year alone, the Bank of Israel was forced to buy $5 billion worth of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The shekel is fixed daily against a basket of five \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated by the dollar, weighted around 60%. \par \par \uc2  The basket also closed unchanged Thursday at 3.6957 shekels. \par \par \uc2  -By Amy Teibel \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 29-05-97 \par \par \uc2  1357GMT \par \par \uc2  1092 1849 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt5t03wz0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
