\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel seen cutting rates nil to 0.3 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Rosenberg \par \uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Feb 23 (Reuter) - Analysts said that the Bank of Israel would either leave interest rates unchanged or else cut them by up to 0.3 percentage points when it announces its March monetary programme on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The analysts on Sunday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces contradictory signals, ranging from a low January consumer price index (CPI) to a widening budget deficit. The daily newspaper Maariv on Sunday said Bank of Israel Governor Jacob Frenkel is under pressure from the prime minister's office and the Treasury to make a big cut. \par \par \uc2  "I think Frenkel is very resistant to this kind of pressure," said Danniella Finn, a vice president at Betucha Securities. "The estimates are for 0.0-0.3 percentage point cut in interest rates....I feel comfortable with those estimates." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel has cut the base rate 0.5 percentage point in each of the last two months, capping a total reduction of 2.8 points since the middle of last year. The base rate is currently 14.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Adam Reuter, managing director of Financial Immunities, a financial consulting firm, predicted Frenkel would lower the rate 0.3 point. \par \par \uc2  "First they (the Bank of Israel) absorbed the liquidity they injected into the economy, second the CPI was low and finally the state of the economy," he said citing reasons for making a relatively sharp cut. \par \par \uc2  In a bid to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket rate against the Israeli shekel, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been routinely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-January. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is successfully absorbing the resulting liquidity. \par \par \uc2  Although, M1 money supply rose a sharp 3.4 percent in January, analysts have dismissed any connection with the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, consumer prices rose a moderate 0.4 percent in January, while the economy remains in a slowdown. \par \par \uc2  Roy Meir, of Simodan brokerage house, said he thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hold rates this month. \par \par \uc2  "The January budget deficit was a lot bigger than expected," he said. "Even though there was a low January consumer price index, that's history. The deficit is still an issue." \par \par \uc2  He said it was unlikely that rates would come down until the Treasury was able to put spending back on course, which he said would not occur before the end of March. \par \par \uc2  Reuter said he also doubted rates would continue coming down after this month. \par \par \uc2  "I think we've reached the end of a trend," he said. "We'll see higher CPIs in March and April because of the strength of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  - Tel Aviv newsroom, 972-3-537-2211 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt2n09mba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
